{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_013QhorNTF13WrYWJqMaKXfz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`answers/01-037-risks-of-general-purpose-tokenizers-on-legalmedical-domains.md`** — Q37: Risks of general-purpose tokenizers on legal/medical domains.\n\nKey content in the note:\n\n- **Root cause**: BPE merges are frequency-learned from web text, so rare domain terms never form stable tokens — `\"warfarin\"` → `[\"war\",\"far\",\"in\"]`, ICD-10 codes split at chemically/legally meaningless boundaries\n- **5 concrete risks**: token inflation (2–4×), context window pressure, semantic confusion, inconsistent capitalization handling, negation mishandling\n- **Cost math**: at GPT-4o pricing + 1M queries/day, fragmentation alone can add ~$3,600/day\n- **Mitigations in priority order**: upstream normalization (RxNorm, legal glossary) → domain-specific embedders (BioLORD, legal-bert) → BM25 hybrid retrieval → domain-adapted tokenizer + retrain as last resort\n- **Verbal script** walks through the fragmentation mechanism with concrete examples, then pivots to pragmatic production fixes\n- **3 pitfalls**: treating tokenization as neutral preprocessing, ignoring cost impact, jumping to retraining before trying normalization\n\n11 items remain in the Next queue (Q38–Q48 + validate)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":381,"cache_read_input_tokens":58152,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":381,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6a3406df-266f-45cc-b881-ccfcc1c87774","uuid":"c3bbb746-ec84-4525-a1af-2549b61a5c02"}
